traces.inference_trace:
The approach is the standard Benchmarks Game algorithm, with the permutation space split across threads.

1. **Factorial number system.** Any permutation index `idx` in `[0, N!)` can be decoded directly into its starting permutation and count array.
   - For `i = N-1` down to `1`, compute the digit `d = idx / i!` and set `idx %= i!`.
   - Store `count[i] = d`, then rotate `perm[0..i]` left by `d`.
   - This lets each thread jump straight to the start of its own block of permutations.

2. **Sequential generation.** Within a block, the next permutation comes from the usual count-based rotation step.
   - Start with `i = 1`. Rotate `perm[0..i]` left by one and increment `count[i]`.
   - If `count[i] <= i`, stop. Otherwise reset `count[i]` to 0, increment `i`, and repeat.
   - This yields exactly the required ordering.

3. **Flip counting.** Copy the permutation. While the first element `f` is not 0, reverse the first `f+1` elements and count one flip.
   - The checksum adds the flip count when the permutation index is even and subtracts it when the index is odd.

4. **Parallelism.** The index range is cut into chunks, which threads take dynamically through an atomic counter. Each thread keeps its own maximum and checksum, and these are merged at the end.

For N = 7 this gives `228` and `Pfannkuchen(7) = 16`.

```cpp
#include <cstdio>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <thread>
#include <vector>
#include <atomic>
#include <algorithm>
#include <cstring>

static int n;
static long long fact[21];
static long long total, chunk;
static std::atomic<long long> nextChunk(0);

struct Res { long long checksum = 0; int maxf = 0; };

static void worker(Res* r) {
    long long cs = 0; int mx = 0;
    while (true) {
        long long start = nextChunk.fetch_add(chunk);
        if (start >= total) break;
        long long end = std::min(total, start + chunk);
        int perm[16], count[16], p[16], tmp[16];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) perm[i] = i;
        long long idx = start;
        for (int i = n - 1; i > 0; i--) {
            long long d = idx / fact[i];
            idx %= fact[i];
            count[i] = (int)d;
            for (int j = 0; j <= i; j++) tmp[j] = perm[j];
            for (int j = 0; j <= i; j++) {
                int k = j + (int)d;
                if (k > i) k -= i + 1;
                perm[j] = tmp[k];
            }
        }
        count[0] = 0;
        for (long long pi = start; pi < end; pi++) {
            if (perm[0] != 0) {
                memcpy(p, perm, sizeof(int) * n);
                int flips = 0;
                int f = p[0];
                while (f != 0) {
                    int lo = 0, hi = f;
                    while (lo < hi) { int t = p[lo]; p[lo] = p[hi]; p[hi] = t; lo++; hi--; }
                    flips++;
                    f = p[0];
                }
                if (flips > mx) mx = flips;
                cs += (pi & 1) ? -flips : flips;
            }
            // next permutation
            int i = 1;
            while (i < n) {
                int first = perm[0];
                for (int j = 0; j < i; j++) perm[j] = perm[j + 1];
                perm[i] = first;
                count[i]++;
                if (count[i] <= i) break;
                count[i] = 0;
                i++;
            }
        }
    }
    r->checksum = cs; r->maxf = mx;
}

